  "body": "**Apply the quality-binding correction in #2984 before machine use or merge.** The controller can resolve different numbered quality names to different resident targets in one group. Plotted offsets alone do not verify that every work selected the requested quality.\n\nStacked on #2972. Review the [multiple-work changes above the parent](https://github.com/qsmits/Camcraft/compare/980ed2af14fef01a6a1129ef973518563ed1d9ac...760e62cc194f49d6efb51cfd557574d7df4cede0).\n\nPost two captured punches from separate retained solids on one 20 mm stock. Check their complete compensated cut and threading envelopes against each other and the stock. Reject a shared source, a hidden additional model, an unsafe lead or an unsupported combination before writing the output directory.\n\nOne native piece file refers to both works. Each has a distinct nominal ISO geometry file, resource, quality selection, threading point and separation. The resource file lists both geometries. Single-work jobs keep their existing file layout.\n\nRun the original pair from the repository root with `cargo run -p camcraft-app --bin camcraft-cli -- script examples/agie/punch-pair.camscript`. Both operations generated without warnings.\n\nAgie Control plotted the two nominal shapes and ran 16 cut blocks. It cut four profile passes for each punch, then four separation passes for each. The nine nominal faces had outward offsets of 0.245, 0.155, 0.140 and 0.134 mm. Each rough separation measured 1.000 mm. The left and right cutting envelopes stayed apart throughout. The formal check reported zero job errors and requested reference setup. It also reported option 131072 for each work. No physical cut was made.\n\nThe independent gate passed all 18 grouped, cutoff, die and native integration tests and all 14 Agie post tests. Strict Clippy passed for the app and post libraries. The final integrated branch reproduced the four controller-tested files byte for byte.\n\nEffort: high\nChangelog: The native Agie post groups two independently captured punches on one 20 mm plate after checking their cut and threading envelopes.\n\nFixes #2961\n\n",
